  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry and quiet weather will prevail through Saturday morning\nunder high pressure. A weak trough across western Pennsylvania \nand shallow instability (600-800J/kg of surface based CAPE) may \nprompt isolated shower activity between 3pm to 7pm Saturday \nafternoon. The potential for lighting is low but not zero. \nUpdrafts may struggle to develop due to entrained dry air. \n\nConfidence is increasing that mesoscale convective systems\n(MCSs) will develop over the Great Lakes this weekend as high-\nresolution model guidance continues to come into range. For\nSunday, the threat for strong to severe storms may stay west of\nour region, more-so in central and western Ohio where a moisture\nboundary will likely reside. If there is a strong to severe\nthunderstorm, it may occur in eastern Ohio due to remnant\noutflow boundaries over Lake Erie or southern Michigan advancing\nsouth. \n\nThe potential for widespread strong to severe storms becomes \nmore favorable Monday into Tuesday as the ridge over the \nnorthern Great Lakes weakens, opening the door for upstream MCSs\nto propagate into the region or that remnant outflow boundaries\nof decaying MCSs redevelopment during peak heating. \n\nIf a mesoscale convective systems (MCSs) does develop and track\ninto our region, forecast soundings indicate an environment \ncapable of supporting all severe weather hazards, including \nhail, damaging wind gusts, tornadoes, and heavy rainfall.\n\nNCAR Machine-learning guidance has a 5-10% of severe weather\nover our region on Sunday; 15 to 45% chance for Monday and\nTuesday. \n\nColorado State University (CSU) machine learning has \n5-15% chance of excessive rainfall (flash flooding) Monday into\nTuesday. The Weather Prediction Center has issued \"Marginal\"\n(1 out of 4) risks for back-to-back days. \n\nThe eventual evolution of this threat will largely depend on \nthe amplitude of the eastern U.S. trough and the strength and \nposition of the upper- level ridge, which will influence the \ntrack and intensity of any MCSs.\n\nThe threat for severe storms and precipitation is expected to\nremain low Wednesday into Thursday in the wake of a departing\ncold front and building high pressure.",
